WEBVTT

00:00:00.175 --> 00:00:03.502
An agent asks to refund an order. Should that request execute?

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:08.262
Before we worry about which model made the request, we need a place where our application can say no.

00:00:08.296 --> 00:00:10.766
In this module, we build that place from scratch.

00:00:10.788 --> 00:00:14.857
A small registry, explicit permissions, a bounded loop, and an event log.

00:00:14.891 --> 00:00:19.583
Everything runs against a scripted model, so you can inspect the behavior without paying for model calls or relying

00:00:19.617 --> 00:00:22.405
on a lucky response. Start with the tool registry.

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:26.291
Each tool has a name, a description, a permission, and a function that runs it.

00:00:26.337 --> 00:00:29.476
Look at the actual output here. Looking up an order is allowed.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:33.284
Issuing a refund requires approval. Deleting a customer is denied.

00:00:33.318 --> 00:00:36.197
That policy lives beside the executable tool definition.

00:00:36.219 --> 00:00:40.481
A description can help the model choose a tool, but a description does not enforce permission.

00:00:40.503 --> 00:00:43.255
The application still has to check before calling the function.

00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:46.214
Now compare the same tools before and after approval.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.912
The refund moves from needs approval to okay.

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:52.584
The denied delete operation stays denied. That distinction matters.

00:00:52.606 --> 00:00:56.335
A generic approved flag should not silently turn every capability on.

00:00:56.369 --> 00:01:00.607
This first module uses a simple set of approved tool names to make the control flow visible.

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:06.024
It is a teaching step. Module three tightens approval so it covers one exact payload, rather than every possible

00:01:06.092 --> 00:01:09.537
invocation of a tool. Next, load the refunds skill.

00:01:09.651 --> 00:01:11.940
Before loading, the visible tool set is empty.

00:01:12.008 --> 00:01:15.374
After loading, the agent can see the order lookup and refund tools.

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:18.093
The skill also brings the refund policy into context.

00:01:18.127 --> 00:01:23.476
We are separating the capabilities the application has from the capabilities this particular task exposes.

00:01:23.522 --> 00:01:25.856
Keep the permission check even when tools are hidden.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:31.200
Reducing what the model sees helps keep context focused, but visibility alone is not authorization.

00:01:31.302 --> 00:01:33.789
The fake model is just a function over context.

00:01:33.811 --> 00:01:37.257
It can request a skill, propose a tool call, or return an answer.

00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:41.745
Because its choices are scripted, the test can deliberately reach the refund boundary every time.

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:44.340
We do not need to persuade a live model to misbehave.

00:01:44.374 --> 00:01:49.632
We can ask a narrower question: when this exact proposal arrives, does the loop enforce the rule?

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.427
Later, a real model can replace the scripted one without taking ownership of that rule.

00:01:53.485 --> 00:01:57.939
Here are the completed runs. Without approval, the lookup succeeds and the refund is blocked.

00:01:57.973 --> 00:02:02.665
With approval, the simulated refund runs. With a two step budget, the loop stops early.

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:07.254
The event log records the difference instead of asking us to infer it from a friendly final answer.

00:02:07.424 --> 00:02:10.586
These are local fake tools, not real customer operations.

00:02:10.756 --> 00:02:13.102
Watch the calls, results, and blocked events.

00:02:13.272 --> 00:02:16.944
Those events are the evidence that tells us which path the application actually took.

00:02:17.148 --> 00:02:20.276
Run the finished example, then work through the three exercises.

00:02:20.344 --> 00:02:22.859
Build a shipping model that only uses the shipping skill.

00:02:22.927 --> 00:02:25.375
Add a tool call budget alongside the step budget.

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:27.891
Finally, summarize a run from its event log.

00:02:27.925 --> 00:02:31.971
The starter exercise tests are supposed to fail until you implement the missing pieces.

00:02:32.039 --> 00:02:36.799
The checkpoint for this module is simple: you should be able to point at the exact line that prevents an unapproved

00:02:36.901 --> 00:02:41.014
tool from running. That is the foundation! Now we can make the boundaries stronger.
